C = [
("h1", "Preface"),
("p", "This volume argues a position. It does not survey the ways the Cross has been explained and leave the choice to the reader. It says what the author believes Paul teaches when he writes about the death of Christ, it gives the evidence from Paul’s own sentences, and it answers the strongest objections in the objectors’ own terms."),
("p", "The position is this. Paul gives no single account of the Cross. Passage by passage he names several things it did, and each is set against a different problem. In Romans 3 the problem is the righteousness of God, and the Cross is the place of propitiation. In Galatians 3 the problem is a curse, and Christ bears it. In 2 Corinthians 5 one dies for all, and the question is what follows for those who live. In Romans 6 and Colossians 2 and 3 the problem is the old man and the world, and the believer is said to have died with Christ. In Colossians 2 the problem is a record that was against us, and the powers. In Ephesians 2 and Colossians 1 the problem is enmity, and the Cross makes peace. These are not six theories among which a reader must choose, and they are not one doctrine said six times. Each is read as the passage states it."),
("p", "Three things about the method. First, the volume reads the King James Version only, and every Scripture quotation is checked word for word against it. Second, the volume asks the same four questions of every passage: what the Cross is said to have done, to what, for whom, and with what result. Where a passage does not answer one of the four, the volume says so and does not borrow the answer from another passage. Third, where modern writers are named, they are named for what they say. A writer is quoted only from a text the author has read in full and that anyone can read for free. Otherwise the position is described in the author’s words and no view is credited to anyone who has not been read."),
("p", "The volume is written for readers who know the passages and want to see them together. It does not require Greek. A few Greek words that carry weight are given in Roman letters in the appendix on key words, with the way the King James Version renders them. The reader is asked to keep a Bible open, to test every row of every table, and to correct the author where the text does."),
("p", "The author does not claim to have said the last word on the central event of the faith. The claim is narrower. When each of Paul’s passages on the Cross is read as answering the question he put to it, the old contest between the explanations loses some of its force, and each passage is free to say what it says."),
("h1", "Thesis"),
("p", "Paul names several things the Cross did, each against a different problem, and the passages are not rival theories and not one doctrine said again."),
("ctbl", "The thesis in four statements.", [
    ("Statement", "What it says", "Where it is argued"),
    ("1", "Paul’s passages on the Cross each answer a different problem: God’s righteousness (Romans 3), the curse (Galatians 3), the death of all (2 Corinthians 5), the old man (Romans 6; Colossians 2 and 3), the record and the powers (Colossians 2), and the enmity (Ephesians 2; Colossians 1).", "Chapters 3 to 10"),
    ("2", "Each passage is read by the same four questions: what the Cross did, to what, for whom, and with what result. The answer comes from the passage and from no other.", "Chapters 1, 13 and 14"),
    ("3", "The words for the Cross are not interchangeable. Propitiation, curse, death with Christ, blotting out, peace and offering do different work in different sentences.", "Chapters 1, 13 and 15"),
    ("4", "The passages are the Body’s own: the gospel Paul preached names the death of Christ as the first thing he delivered. They do not make the Body a party to Israel’s covenants, and they do not tell what earlier believers were saved by.", "Chapters 2, 5, 9 and 15"),
  ], [0.15, 0.57, 0.28]),
("p", "A reader may test the thesis in one move. Take any two of the passages and ask whether they answer the same problem. If they do, the passages may be one doctrine said twice and the thesis fails. If they answer different problems, the thesis stands, and the reader will have seen why Paul never gives the Cross a single name."),
]
